WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.375
Who has to be involved.

2
00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:05.038
This is an organizational change
as much as a technical one.

3
00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:05.887
Owners.

4
00:00:05.887 --> 00:00:09.888
HR owns the personnel data and
its quality, at the source.

5
00:00:09.888 --> 00:00:16.225
The OPM HR 2.0 program owns the platform,
the standards and the interconnection.

6
00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:18.875
And each contractor source
owns its own record.

7
00:00:18.875 --> 00:00:19.775
Users.

8
00:00:19.775 --> 00:00:25.850
The CIO's identity team runs Entra ID,
Active Directory and access from that data.

9
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:30.125
Security sets the Zero Trust
policy and owns the leaver path.

10
00:00:30.125 --> 00:00:34.162
And the help desk moves from routine
tickets to handling exceptions.

11
00:00:34.162 --> 00:00:34.975
Guards.

12
00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:37.475
The privacy office is in from the start.

13
00:00:37.475 --> 00:00:42.652
Sharing HR records with identity inside
the agency normally rests on the Privacy

14
00:00:42.652 --> 00:00:44.403
Act's need-to-know provision,

15
00:00:44.403 --> 00:00:47.982
while disclosures outside the
agency need a routine use,

16
00:00:47.982 --> 00:00:51.255
so the privacy office decides
the basis for each leg,

17
00:00:51.255 --> 00:00:55.062
along with the privacy impact
assessment and minimization.

18
00:00:55.062 --> 00:00:58.225
The PIV office owns
issuance and termination.

19
00:00:58.225 --> 00:01:04.150
And the ISSO and authorizing official
own the evidence and any accepted risk.

20
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.660
The data architect ties it
together: owning the standards,

21
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.632
the crosswalk and the person key, making
sure every attribute has one accountable

22
00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:17.380
owner, and carrying the standard to the
interagency forums where it's adopted.
